# Copy to `.env` (gitignored) and fill in.
#
# This key is used ONLY by scripts/probe.mjs, which runs in Node.
# The app itself never reads it: you paste your key into the header field in
# the UI, where it is stored in localStorage under AES-GCM.
#
# Deliberately NOT prefixed with VITE_. Vite only exposes VITE_-prefixed
# variables to client code, so an unprefixed name cannot end up inlined into
# the browser bundle.
#
# Use a key separate from any other tooling, so it can be revoked on its own.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Where the local heylook server is, as an origin with a port.
#
# Unlike the key above, this one IS read by the app -- that is what the VITE_
# prefix means, and it is safe here because a hostname is not a secret and has
# to reach the browser to be of any use.
#
# It is also written into the page's Content-Security-Policy at build time, so
# the host the policy allows and the host the client calls are one value. That
# means a change here needs a restart (`bun run dev`) to take effect, and that
# an origin the policy does not name is refused by the browser with no status
# and no response.
#
# Leave it unset to use http://localhost:8000, which is heylook's default.
# A plain http:// origin that is not localhost is blocked as mixed content if
# this app is ever served over https; over the http dev server both work.
VITE_HEYLOOK_ORIGIN=http://localhost:8000

# Several machines, if you run heylook on more than one.
#
# A comma-separated list of name=origin. When set it replaces the single origin
# above and every listed host goes into connect-src. The picker in the header
# appears only when the list has more than one entry, so a single-entry list
# looks like it did nothing. The names are yours -- nothing resolves behaviour
# by matching one.
#
# Why here and not in the UI: which hosts may be contacted at all is decided by
# the page's Content-Security-Policy, which is generated from this list at build
# time. How to treat a machine once it is listed -- how slow it is, how long to
# queue for it -- is a runtime setting, because getting that wrong costs a slow
# call rather than a hole in the policy.
#
# Put these in a .env file rather than on the command line. Vite's config and
# the browser bundle read env through different mechanisms, and a variable given
# only on the command line has reached one and not the other.
# VITE_HEYLOOK_INSTANCES=<name>=http://localhost:8000,<other>=http://<host>:8000

# That is the whole surface. Everything else the app can be told -- which
# provider to use, which model, whether to constrain decoding, and the
# per-machine policy overrides -- is runtime state, stored in the browser and
# set in the UI. The split is deliberate and it is in `src/provider/registry.ts`:
# build time decides which hosts may be contacted at all, because the page's
# Content-Security-Policy is generated from the same values; run time decides
# how to treat a host once it is listed. A second place to set a runtime value
# would be a second thing to disagree with the one the UI writes.
#
# The Gemini endpoint and model id are constants, not variables: DEFAULT_MODEL
# in `src/provider/gemini.ts`.
